Scheduling benchmark runs

Run timing benchmarks with:
  cargo bench -p eqlog-bench --bench scheduling

Run allocation counting separately with:
  cargo bench -p eqlog-bench --bench scheduling --features allocations

The harness writes CSV to stdout. EQLOG_BENCH_FILTER selects one case by
name; EQLOG_BENCH_MODE selects simultaneous or stratified. The defaults
are EQLOG_BENCH_SCALE=8, EQLOG_BENCH_SAMPLES=15, EQLOG_BENCH_WARMUP=2.
Each sample constructs a fresh model. Only close() is measured; setup,
input edits, result validation, and destruction are excluded. Incremental
and unchanged samples first close their baseline outside measurement.
All runs use semi-naive matching and native morphism propagation.

At scale 8:
  pipeline: 24 stages, 512 initial items; update adds 8 items.
  independent: 16 two-rule components, 128 items each; update adds 8
    items to one component.
  model_chain/model_cycle: 16 models, 8 consecutive edges per model,
    two disjoint paths of 129 items. Initially seed the first path at
    model 0; update seeds the second path at model 8. Earlier edge
    blocks are inherited, so the update can follow the second path
    from its beginning.
  nested: 4 parents in a chain, each with a chain of 4 children and
    8 consecutive edges per child; parent morphisms map corresponding
    children. Initially seed a 33-item path at parent 0; update seeds
    a separate 33-item path at the last parent.
  equality: 1024 key/value pairs with distinct lookup values. Initially
    merge 512 pairs of values to enable lookups; update merges 32 more.
Unchanged samples call close() again without edits.

The allocation build reports successful allocation and reallocation
counts and total requested bytes. Reallocation counts the full new
size. These numbers describe allocation traffic, not live or peak
memory. Timing builds use the normal allocator without counters.

Recorded environment:
  2026-10-17, x86_64 Linux container
  AMD EPYC 9645 96-Core Processor; process pinned to logical CPU 0
  Rust 1.95.0, Cargo bench profile, default optimization settings
  Current evaluator: 1dba1f0908197ed99afc396e8d2818bb503b4f3c
  Historical evaluator: db41b3eeb8bf531d39525f8cd0d703431bf13bd8
  Benchmark harness: 5e79220

The same benchmark package was copied into a detached worktree at
db41b3e and added to that workspace. Only its simultaneous mode was
selected. Release binaries were built before measurement and ran
serially, pinned with taskset -c 0. Each timing CSV reports medians
and sample p10/p90 values, not confidence intervals.

Allocation CSVs use the same scale, three samples, and no warmup
(EQLOG_BENCH_SAMPLES=3 EQLOG_BENCH_WARMUP=0). The historical runs also
set EQLOG_BENCH_MODE=simultaneous.

These runs identify three follow-ups before changing the default:
  - Unchanged model chains still take 12.0 ms and 253,628 allocations
    with stratified scheduling, versus 0.89 ms and 20,798 allocations
    with current simultaneous scheduling.
  - Current simultaneous initial closure regresses against the old
    evaluator on model chains (75.5 vs 27.9 ms), cycles (444 vs 282 ms),
    and nested models (12.5 vs 6.68 ms). Stratified initial closure
    beats both simultaneous versions on those cases.
  - Small equality updates take 0.57 ms stratified and 0.71 ms with
    current simultaneous scheduling, versus 0.20 ms historically.

The ordinary pipeline and independent components have similar initial
closure times in the two current schedules. The large graph gains do
not establish an advantage for stratification on every workload.
